1075 #edb/ref 17592186359659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360429 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360429 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360429 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186358729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360429 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f550e8b8-0dda-5ff3-ba5c-bfed41503899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360554 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360554 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360554 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360554 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)={e}^{x+2y},x={s}^{2},$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360554 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40900f6f-b4af-5c59-b5c2-386989d530fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360554 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x+2y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{e}^{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & ={e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}\\cdot 2s+2{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2s{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}+2{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s} \\\\  & =2(s+1){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360558 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y}$ and $y={e}^{-x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d95d5a8a-ad5f-52dc-beb9-600c878518d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360558 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}\\cdot (-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}+\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360566 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360566 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360566 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360566 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360566 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e7ed0c3-433c-5b76-94b3-8ee539ba166f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360566 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383615 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383615 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383615 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383618