ef 17592186524107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524041 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03875695-6306-58ee-ade0-e90c339315ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524041 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186360569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524044 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524044 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1cab00d5-4da8-576c-bf80-814a66279e8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524044 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186360551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524052 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a017487-80b3-5b03-a1d5-2934bfa2d036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524052 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186360571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524081 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524081 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9e72f9a3-b2cc-52fb-b449-d5acf347acc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524081 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186360555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524107 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524107 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fac2284d-62e5-5d58-b0c1-c75c972f080b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524107 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186360570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526410 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186526392 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526410 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526410 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=- x^{2} + x y$ and $y=- \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526410 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e7676fd-d001-5de3-b465-0aeb6e24740a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526410 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 2 x + y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =- 2 x - \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 x - \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}+\\left(x\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 4 \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =- 4 x \\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)} - 2 x - \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526417 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186526422 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526417 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526417 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=- x^{2} - 2 x y$ and $y=- \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526417 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72cc3095-5d1d-534f-8f85-48cfe2429d46" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526417 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 2 x - 2 y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =- 2 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =- 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =4 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 2 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\  & =2 \\left(2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - x + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526421 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186526412 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526421 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526421 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + x y$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526421 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d1cf1af-73f0-5835-a9c0-2ae46945e41a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186526421 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=8 x + y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =8 x + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =8 x + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+\\left(x\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} + 8 x + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443]}
